From 962b25c5d666c81148174677b73f9fad909d9e03 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 08:32:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Water that answers what moves through it: wakes, rings and
 splashes under the new look

Corey's reference (a retail pond seen from above) shows what our water
lacked: a swimmer leaves thin, crisp white streaks fanning out behind
him in a V, a hero wading in sends rings out and splashes where he
steps in, the open water moves as a sheet (a fine grain of small
wavelets over soft larger swells, drifting gently, no specks), the bed
wavers under the moving surface, the shore thins to clear water with a
faint pale edge, and the shallows show their sand plainly, with no
glitter over them.

The wakes. A ripple field of 320 x 320 texels over 80 yd, centred on
our own body (a little ahead of the camera with none), snapped to whole
texels, is stepped on the GPU each frame (look_wakes.wgsl, one compute
pass, look_wakes in the fps journal's gpu_look column and named on its
GPU_PASSES line) as a linear shallow-water wave at fixed 1/120 s steps,
in pairs. Every unit with its feet in the water pushes on it by its
speed, smoothed over 0.12 s (a still one only breathes gentle rings),
one coming in splashes (harder landing from a hop or a fall), one
stopping sends its bow wave on as a ring and settles in rings for about
a second, a fishing bobber nods, and raindrops dimple it. Nothing pushes
within 3 yd of the field's edge, and a body's push grows in over the
next 4 yd; the water fades the field in over the same yards.

The V. Behind a moving body two arms trail from its sides at 19.5
degrees either side of its track, as a Kelvin wake's do at any speed,
reaching back as far as it goes in 1.2 s. They push on the water and lay
foam along them, and their foam is carried outward as fast as the arms
move, so it stays on them; it fades over about a second and a half.
Each arm's foam is broken into streaks with gaps by a smooth noise along
the body's track, over stretches of 0.6 yd, which a streak keeps as the
arm carries it outward. Foam is born only there, at a swimmer's sides
and as a splash's few droplets: a still body, a ring, a raindrop or a
wader's shins (under 0.4 yd, fully from 0.9) never foam, a texel takes
at most 12 a second, and new foam is held back as the share of the
field under foam nears 4% (read back from the GPU).

The water draws the foam as thin near-white lines along its ridges:
where the field's foam curves down most steeply (the Hessian's lower
eigenvalue), within about a third of a texel of the ridge (a Newton
step), to two-thirds where it is thick and fresh, softening over half a
texel, so a streak is a hand's width across however far its foam has
spread as it is carried. A streak hides 0.7 of the water, never all of
it, with a faint soft halo (0.12) round it, so it fades rather than
blinks out; it is dimmed only by how bright the light is, never tinted
by its colour. The rings show from above as faint pale lines along the
field's crests (where its height curves down, read two texels either
side so a raindrop's texel does not read as one), a quarter as strong
in full rain. The field's slopes add to the water's own ripples, so the
mirror, the glint and the bed move with it.

They replace 0081's wading froth and 1.12's own wade wake and rings
(water_fx), which drew an older ring over them: the ring round a
standing hero's neck that four films found is 1.12's, and it no longer
draws while the wakes run. `lookwater wakes 0` brings both back.

The open water's surface under the wakes (look::water_surface): four
layers of gradient-noise wavelets, 3.2, 1.28, 0.36 and 0.2 yd long,
drift along the wind (look::wind's downwind, each turned off it by 0,
35, -55 and 120 degrees, so the sheet never slides one way) at 0.6, 0.9,
0.45 and 0.55 yd/s, weighted 0.5, 0.45, 0.3 and 0.6 (0.7 to 1.3 of that as the wind strengthens), each only
sliding, so nothing pops; their crossing keeps the pattern changing. A compute pass, look_water_surface (the fps
journal's gpu_look, named on its GPU_PASSES line), bakes them once a
frame into one 25.6 yd tile of 512 x 512 texels (the long pair's slope
and the short pair's), each layer periodic over the tile and its drift
kept on the CPU in whole cells modulo it, so the tile never jumps. The
water reads it by texel (wrapped), its slope the hour's ripple slope
times 0.8 (about 0.1 by day), varied 0.7 to 1.3 by an untiled swell of
about 12 yd so the repeat never shows. The short pair fades as a pixel
grows from 0.04 to 0.14 yd, the long from 0.3 to 1; what fades is handed
to the sun's highlight as roughness, which widens and dims it. The
highlight off it is broader (sharpness 320, at 0.35 of 0081's, the grain
counting half) and never brighter than 1.6, so a crest is a soft streak,
not a speck; the brightness of all the water (the bed seen through it
too) follows the wavelets, a facet toward the sun's bearing brighter
and away darker, by 0.04 for the long pair and 0.1 for the grain at
twice each pair's own mean slope, half of that in shade, so its drift
moves the sheet without reading as an embossed relief. A fine grain of
about 0.1 yd lies over it, per pixel (the map's 0.05 yd texels cannot
hold it): two octaves (0.1 and 0.06 yd) of the bake's gradient noise,
periodic over the tile, drifting near still (0.04 yd/s, the finer
octave at 0.85 of it: at a capillary ripple's 0.3 yd/s the octaves moved
a tenth and a seventh of their wavelength each 30th of a second, along
crossing ways, so the grain boiled, its frame-to-frame change on film
most of its texture), each faded out as a pixel grows from a quarter to
0.6 of its wavelength; it shades the water by 0.45 at its mean tilt
toward the sun, tilts the surface by that share of the slope and bends
the bed. At
the films' camera (1920x1080, 0.0135 yd a pixel) it lies at 4 to 7 px,
where the reference's fine texture is; the finest baked wavelet (0.2 yd)
lies at 15 px; the sky's mirror is
Fresnel's (0.1 looking straight down), faint looking down and strong
toward the horizon, its clouds mirrored with an edge four times softer
and blurred to their broadest octave, and four-fifths of it kept in
shade. 1.12's
ripple flipbook and its sparkle (the white flecks over the whole lake on
the fourth filming) are gone under it.

The bed through moving water: the bed seen under the water is drawn back
from the surface's tilt, 3.5 yd per unit of slope per yard of depth to
1.5 yd deep, so it wavers under the ripples and the wakes (under the
drifting wavelets the long pair bends it at 1.1 of that and the grain at
0.4, so it wobbles with nobody swimming); faint moving light lies on the
shallow bed in the sun (under the wavelets 1.6 times as strong and three
times as quick). Where the bend is under a
pixel the frame's blend keeps the bed it already holds, so a calm
surface still shows what is drawn behind it.

Clear shallows under the wakes: the water takes the bed's light by
colour, red first (0.44, 0.19 and 0.17 of it a yard of the view's path),
so the bed shows plainly through the first yard and a half, tinted
toward teal, and the colour deepens below; the frame's blend keeps of
the bed behind only what every channel lets through and the water lays
the rest from the scene copy. The sun's glint and 1.12's own sparkle
(its ocean0_s.bls sheen over the ripple frame's specks, which speckled
the whole of the shallows pink-white on film, with the wakes on or off)
are cut to 0.04 under 1.5 yd and come back to full by 3 yd; the sparkle
is laid over the water instead of inside its body (whose colour by
channel tinted it pink), near-white, and only at the frame's brightest
specks, so they are smaller; the glint is near-white too. All of this
only with the wakes on, so `lookwater wakes 0`, `worldWater 0` and
`worldLook 0` draw the water exactly as before.

Pale, milky shallows under the drifting wavelets: Corey's reference pond
is pale and milky green (about 127 of luminance) where the fifth filming's
lake was dark (about 50). Over the view's path through the water the
bed's light gives way to the water's own, coming in over about 0.9 yd
of path and none at the waterline, and fully only past 1.2 yd of depth,
so the bed shows plainly under the first yard and through a pale veil
beyond. How much it takes is shaped by depth: up to 0.65 of the bed's
light in the shallows (to 1 yd deep), 0.3 at 2.5 yd and 0.65 from 4.5
yd, turning smoothly between (one strength, 0.35, brightened the middle
depths, whose body is the pale shallow colour over a dark bed, before
the deep and the shallows: on film 104 to 149 against 105 to 124 and 96
to 129 for 0 to 0.7), for about 127, 130 and 122 at noon. The water takes 0.6 of the hour's absorption, so
the bed shows plainly; the milky water is lit by the hour's ambient and
0.85 of its sun even in shade (light scattered in from the lit water
round it); the shallow colour goes most of the way (0.85) to a pale,
slightly warm green (sRGB 0.60, 0.76, 0.68) at 0.85 of its brightness,
the deep colour 0.3 of the way to it at its own, and it turns to the deep from 1 yd to 5 (1.2 to 3.5 drew a pale band down a shelf's middle
depths); the shore's haze is 0.4 of the classic surface's, spread over
twice the depth, so no pale band runs along the shore; and the eight-bit
colour is dithered by half a step so the pale water's broad gradients
turn without bands.

The shore and the shallows, retuned toward the reference: no foam line,
fully clear at the waterline with a faint pale haze over its first
0.7 yd, clearer water (1.8 yd to a third, was 1.4), a greener shallow
colour that holds to 1.2 yd and turns to the deep by 3.5.

A shelf in the bed, blended: the fourth filming found a straight,
blocky-toothed line at about 45 degrees fixed on Crystal Lake (with the
wakes off too), dark clear water over the bed on the near side, lighter
opaque blue-green beyond. The water's colour and clearness follow the
bed's depth pixel by pixel, so where the lake bed drops off a shelf they
turned along 1.12's terrain triangles, whose edges run on the diagonal
two yards a step. Under the wakes they take the bed's depth averaged
over 2.5 yd (8 taps round the pixel, not on it, in opposite pairs,
turned smoothly over the surface, so an even slope reads the pixel's own
depth whatever their turn: one spiral of taps, its centre off the pixel,
drew swirling contours across `show clear`; none on what
stands in front of the water, none shallower than half the pixel's own,
so a swimmer's legs clear no halo), from 0.3 yd deep, fully from 1
(the waterline stays crisp), so the turn spreads over a few yards.

The sun shadow on the water under the drifting wavelets: the fifth
filming's straight diagonal seam was the shade's hand-over between two
shadow slices (the water took the nearer slice's shade to a line of one
eye depth and none past it). A point off a slice now falls through to
the next and a slice's last share blends into it, as the ground's do;
the shade's edge is feathered over 3.5 yd (12 taps), and the water takes
half of the hour's shade, since the in-scatter and the sky still light
it. With the wakes off the water's shadow is as before. `lookwater show <depth|shadow|clear|bed|off>` draws,
under the wakes, what the water is drawn from in its place, for film:
how deep it stands (with the smoothed depth's difference in red), the
sun's share at the surface, how much of the bed comes through, and the
bed as the scene copy holds it.

Rain on the drifting wavelets: drops ring in cells of 0.9 yd, three
offset layers, drops in a share of the cells (0.8 of 0.25 plus 0.6 of
the rain: 0.58 at a rain of 0.8), one ring a second in each cell with
drops, each growing
to about 0.38 yd and 0.09 yd across over that second, kept until a
pixel spans 0.1 yd; they tilt the surface and lay a pale line, 0.8 of
the way to a pale white at a ring's birth, fading with its age, and
the crest's sun side is brighter and its far side darker by 0.3 of
that (1.12's rings, a tenth as wide and gone from a pixel of 0.035 yd,
stay with the wakes off).

A body that jumps (a teleport: over 3 yd in a frame, or over a yard
across at more than 25 yd/s, as the mover spreads one over frames)
leaves no streak, and for half a second after pushes nothing, lays no
foam and comes into the water without a splash.

Switches: worldWater 0 and worldLook 0 run 1.12's water and wade foam
untouched. `lookwater wakes [0|1]` (with no value it reports the bodies
pushing, the field's size, the share of it under foam read back from the
GPU, our own speed as the wakes measure it and as the mover gives it,
how deep our feet are, and the fastest body's speed and depth, each with
the most since the last report, which then starts over; the same line
is logged every 5 s while something moves the water), `lookwater refract <0-2>`
and `lookwater caustics <0-2>` hold the bend and the light, `grade` lets
go, and `lookwater show` is above. `lookwater tune <knob> <value>` holds
one of the open water's main numbers for tuning on film (milk_shallow,
milk_mid, milk_deep, milk_depth, milk_tone, deep_tint, absorb, coarse,
mid, fine, relief, relief_long, grain, grain_yd, grain_speed, sky,
sheen, rain, rain_size, rain_rate, rain_density; the defaults are the
tuning above; `tune milk <value>` sets the three milk strengths
together), `tune <knob> grade` or `tune grade` lets it or all go, and
`tune` alone prints them all; each change is
logged ("[look water] tune set: ..."), so a film's frames can be matched
to the values. They reach only the surface under the wakes.

For the visual check, `probemove <seconds> <f|b|l|r|fl|fr|bl|br>
[facing]`, a dev console command: our body holds those keys for that
long at its swim speed, wading or swimming, turned to that map facing
first (0 north, 90 west) when given; `probemove stop` lets go. A script
can't press the movement keys, and the action layer's dash stops as the
hero starts to swim, so a scripted swim went about 9 yd. Nothing a
player does runs through it.

