+//! The open water's moving surface under the wakes, benilla's own (no reference behaviour).
+//!
+//! Corey's reference pond (a retail lake seen from steeply above) moves as a sheet: a fine, even
+//! grain of small wavelets and soft, larger swells drifting gently with the wind, the bed wavering
+//! under them, a broad soft sheen and no specks. Ours under the fourth round's wakes still drew
+//! 1.12's look: its ripple flipbook (30 frames, flipped at 24 fps, so it pops from frame to frame)
+//! and its sparkle (the sun sheen times the frame's alpha) as white flecks all over the lake, over
+//! two octaves of noise scrolling on the clock. Under the wakes the surface is now this module's:
+//!
+//! 1. **four layers of drifting wavelets** ([`tune::CELLS`]: 3.2, 1.28, 0.36 and 0.2 yd), smooth
+//!    gradient noise each, drifting along the wind (`look::wind`, downwind, each turned a little
+//!    off it, [`tune::TURN_DEG`]) at a gentle pace ([`tune::SPEED_YD`]); a layer only ever slides,
+//!    continuously, so nothing pops, and the layers crossing each other at their own angles and
+//!    speeds keep the pattern changing;
+//! 2. **baked once a frame** into one tile of [`tune::TILE_YD`] ([`SURFACE_MAP`], [`tune::MAP`]
+//!    texels a side: the long pair's slope in `xy`, the short pair's in `zw`) by a compute pass,
+//!    `look_water_surface` (`look_water_surface.wgsl`), in the fps journal's `gpu_look` column and
+//!    named on its `GPU_PASSES` line; every layer is periodic over the tile (whole cells, wrapped),
+//!    and its drift is kept on the CPU in whole cells modulo the tile ([`SurfaceDrift`]), so the
+//!    tile never jumps however long the session runs;
+//! 3. **read by the water** (`liquid.wgsl`): the short pair fades out where a pixel spans its
+//!    wavelets and the long pair further off; what fades is handed to the sun's highlight as
+//!    roughness (it widens and dims rather than flickering into specks); a slow, untiled swell
+//!    over about 12 yd varies their strength so the tile never shows;
+//! 4. **pale and moving** (the sixth round, against the fifth filming's dark, near-still lake):
+//!    the shallows take a milky in-scatter that veils the bed in pale green ([`tune::MILK_SHALLOW`]), the
+//!    wavelets shade the whole of the water's light, the grain by its own share
+//!    ([`tune::SHADE_GRAIN`]), the bed bends under the grain too, and the sun shadow on the water
+//!    is feathered over a few yards at half strength ([`tune::SHADOW`]);
+//! 5. **paler, finer and even** (the seventh round, against the sixth filming's lake: still 25 to
+//!    40% too dark, a bumpy hammered relief, and too little fine texture): the milky in-scatter is
+//!    paler and keeps most of its light in shade ([`tune::MILK_KEEP`]), the deep water takes some
+//!    of it too ([`tune::DEEP_MILK`]), the bed shows through more ([`tune::CLEAR`]), the sky's
+//!    mirror is a little stronger, holds in shade and mirrors the clouds softly
+//!    ([`tune::SKY_SOFT`], [`tune::SKY_BLUR`]); the two long layers are cut to half and the
+//!    0.56 yd layer is now a 0.36 yd grain, even with the finest, both slower;
+//! 6. **a fine grain and a clear shallow bed, tuned on film** (the eighth round, against the
+//!    seventh filming: shallows 155 against the reference's ~130 and the bed hidden; fine texture
+//!    0.9 to 1.7 against 6 to 9; an embossed, crinkled relief; no rain rings to see). The film's
+//!    spectrum told why the grain was missing: the client draws 1920x1080 (1280x720 at a scale of
+//!    1.5), 0.0135 yd a pixel at the films' camera, so the finest wavelet (0.2 yd) lay at 15 px and
+//!    the 0.36 yd one at 26 px; the film held 3.1 of luminance at 16 to 32 px and 0.5 at 2 to 8,
+//!    where the reference holds 6 (the CPU model drew 0.022 yd a pixel, so its 0.2 yd wavelet fell
+//!    under its 5 px measure). Most of the reference's fine texture is its bed, through clear
+//!    water. Neither the map's filtering (read by texel, no mips, faded only past 0.04 yd a pixel)
+//!    nor the video's encode (the reference went through the same) took it. So: a per-pixel grain
+//!    of about 0.1 yd ([`grain_at`], two octaves, periodic over the tile, faded by the pixel's
+//!    span), the 0.36 yd layer and the grain's relief turned down, the milky veil held off the
+//!    first yard or two of depth ([`k::MILK_DEPTH`]) so the bed shows, a darker milk in the
+//!    shallows, and rain rings with a crest and a shaded side. Its main numbers are knobs
+//!    ([`KNOBS`], `lookwater tune <name> <value>`), so a film can step through them and the right
+//!    ones be chosen from the client itself; each change is logged.
+//! 7. **a still grain and a milk shaped by depth** (the ninth round, against the eighth filming's
+//!    sweep: the grain's change from one 30th of a second to the next was about 1.6 times its fine
+//!    texture, where the reference's is about a quarter; and the milk brightened the middle
+//!    depths before the deep and the shallows). The grain only ever slid, never re-randomised,
+//!    but at 0.3 yd/s its 0.1 and 0.06 yd octaves moved a tenth and a seventh of their wavelength
+//!    each frame, along crossing ways, so the pattern boiled: it now drifts at
+//!    [`tune::GRAIN_SPEED`] (`grain_speed`, 0.04 yd/s, near still: under a hundredth of a
+//!    wavelength a frame), so its texture can rise ([`tune::GRAIN`], 0.45) without shimmer. The
+//!    veil's strength is three knobs, at the shallows, the middle depths and the deep
+//!    ([`tune::MILK_AT_YD`]), so each can be brought to the reference's brightness; the rain rings
+//!    are a little sparser ([`tune::RAIN_DENSITY`]).
+//!
+//! `lookwater wakes 0`, `worldWater 0` and `worldLook 0` never bake it and draw the water as before;
+//! the knobs reach only this surface.
+
+use bevy::math::DVec2;
+use bevy::prelude::*;
+use bevy::render::diagnostic::RecordDiagnostics;
+use bevy::render::render_graph::{
+    Node, NodeRunError, RenderGraph, RenderGraphContext, RenderLabel,
+};
+use bevy::render::render_resource::binding_types::{texture_storage_2d, uniform_buffer_sized};
+use bevy::render::render_resource::*;
+use bevy::render::renderer::{RenderContext, RenderDevice, RenderQueue};
+use bevy::render::texture::GpuImage;
+use bevy::render::{render_asset::RenderAssets, Render, RenderApp, RenderStartup, RenderSystems};
+
+use benilla_assets::materials::{surface_map_image, SURFACE_MAP};
+
+use super::water::{WaterFrame, SURFACE_ROW};
+
+/// Every tunable of the surface, mirrored in `look_water_surface.wgsl` (the bake) and
+/// `liquid.wgsl` (the read); `look::water`'s test compares them.
+pub mod tune {
+    /// The tile's side (yd) and its texels a side: 0.05 yd a texel, four to the finest wavelet.
+    pub const TILE_YD: f32 = 25.6;
+    pub const MAP: u32 = 512;
+    /// Each layer's noise cells across the tile: wavelengths of 3.2, 1.28, 0.356 and 0.2 yd. Whole
+    /// cells, so each layer wraps at the tile's edge. Seventh round: the third was 0.557 yd (46),
+    /// whose dents, shaded by the grain's share, read as a hammered relief on the sixth filming.
+    pub const CELLS: [u32; 4] = [8, 20, 72, 128];
+    /// Each layer's share of the slope. Seventh round: the long pair cut to half (were 1.0 and
+    /// 0.8) and the grain even and stronger (were 0.5 and 0.3): the sixth filming's fine texture
+    /// was about 2 against the reference's 5 to 8.
+    pub const WEIGHT: [f32; 4] = [0.5, 0.45, 0.6, 0.6];
+    /// How fast each layer drifts (yd/s) and how far it is turned off downwind (degrees), crossing
+    /// each other, the finest well across the wind, so the sheet never slides one way as a
+    /// conveyor. Sixth round: the fifth filming's lake changed about 14 times less from frame to
+    /// frame than the reference pond (0.11 against 1.52 of luminance at 30 fps), so the 0.56 and
+    /// 0.2 yd wavelets go at 1.1 and 1.0 yd/s (were 0.5 and 0.6), about a wave's own pace at that
+    /// length, and the longer two a little quicker (0.5 and 0.8, were 0.3 and 0.4); turned 0, 35,
+    /// -55 and 120 (were 0, 30, -40 and 75). Seventh round: the long pair a little quicker (0.6
+    /// and 0.9) and the grain slower (0.45 and 0.55), so the stronger, finer grain keeps the
+    /// sixth filming's change from frame to frame (about 1 of luminance at 30 fps).
+    pub const SPEED_YD: [f32; 4] = [0.6, 0.9, 0.45, 0.55];
+    pub const TURN_DEG: [f32; 4] = [0.0, 35.0, -55.0, 120.0];
+    /// The gradient noise's slope per cell, root mean square, per axis (measured: 0.625).
+    pub const NOISE_RMS: f32 = 0.625;
+    /// The wind's strength over which the drift runs from [`DRIFT_CALM`] to [`DRIFT_GUSTY`] of
+    /// its pace; with `worldWind 0` (no wind) it drifts along [`STILL_DIR`] at [`DRIFT_CALM`].
+    pub const DRIFT_CALM: f32 = 0.7;
+    pub const DRIFT_GUSTY: f32 = 1.3;
+    pub const STILL_DIR: [f32; 2] = [0.8, 0.6];
+    /// The surface's root-mean-square slope as a share of the hour's ripple slope
+    /// (`look::water::ripple_slope`: 0.05 calm, 0.13 a day's breeze): about 0.1 by day (0.55,
+    /// about 0.07, was too faint to see on the fifth filming).
+    pub const GAIN: f32 = 0.8;
+    /// The untiled swell: its scale (yd), drift (yd/s) and how far it varies the slope (0.7 to
+    /// 1.3 of it), so the tile's repeat never shows and soft broad patches drift across.
+    pub const SWELL_YD: f32 = 12.0;
+    pub const SWELL_SPEED: f32 = 0.15;
+    pub const SWELL_VARY: f32 = 0.6;
+    /// The short pair fades out as a pixel grows over this span (yd), the long pair over the next:
+    /// past a quarter of a wavelet's length a pixel would shimmer.
+    pub const FINE_FADE_YD: [f32; 2] = [0.04, 0.14];
+    pub const COARSE_FADE_YD: [f32; 2] = [0.3, 1.0];
+    /// The sun's highlight off this surface: broader and softer than 0081's (900, at 6), so a
+    /// wavelet's crest is a soft streak, never a speck; at most [`GLINT_MOST`] (linear light).
+    /// What the fades leave out is handed to it as roughness, which widens and dims it.
+    pub const SHARPNESS: f32 = 320.0;
+    pub const GLINT: f32 = 0.35;
+    pub const GLINT_MOST: f32 = 1.6;
+    /// The short pair's share in the highlight's normal: the grain shades the water but its
+    /// steepest tips do not each catch the sun.
+    /// Seventh round 0.25 (was 0.5): the grain now carries most of the slope, and at half of it
+    /// its tips caught the sun as specks again (2.5 times their surroundings).
+    pub const GLINT_FINE: f32 = 0.25;
+    /// The water's brightness varies with the wavelets, all of it (the bed seen through it too): a
+    /// facet turned toward the sun's bearing brighter (and away, darker), at twice its pair's mean
+    /// slope by this much, the long pair [`SHADE`] and the grain [`SHADE_GRAIN`]; half of it in
+    /// shade. The fifth filming's fine texture was 3.6 against the reference's 9.9 (and mostly the
+    /// bed's leaves): the grain now shows (was 0.12 of the body alone, the grain inside it).
+    /// Seventh round: the long pair's 0.08 (was 0.15) and the grain's 0.5 (was 0.45), so the
+    /// swells no longer read as a relief and the grain is the sheet's texture. Eighth round:
+    /// knobs, `relief_long` and `relief` ([`super::KNOBS`]), 0.04 and 0.2: the seventh filming's
+    /// surface read as an embossed relief. Ninth round: `relief` 0.1, the eighth filming's best.
+    pub const SHADE: f32 = 0.04;
+    pub const SHADE_GRAIN: f32 = 0.1;
+    /// The bed's bend from the wavelets, a share of the wakes' (`look::water::tune::REFRACT`): the
+    /// bed wobbles even when nobody swims, under the long pair ([`BEND`], was 0.6) and the grain
+    /// ([`BEND_GRAIN`], was none).
+    pub const BEND: f32 = 1.1;
+    pub const BEND_GRAIN: f32 = 0.4;
+    /// The milky shallows: the fifth filming's lake was dark (about 50 of luminance) where the
+    /// reference pond is pale and milky green (about 127). Over the view's path through the water
+    /// the bed's light gives way to the water's own, at most [`MILK`] of it, coming in over
+    /// [`MILK_YD`] of path (none at the waterline); the shallow colour goes [`MILK_MIX`] of the way
+    /// to [`MILK_RGB`] (sRGB), a pale green a little warm, and turns to the deep from
+    /// [`DEEP_FROM`] to [`DEEP_AT`] (the classic surface's 1.2 to 3.5 drew a pale band down the
+    /// shelf's middle depths). A little more of the sky's mirror looking down ([`F0`], 0.04 else).
+    ///
+    /// Seventh round, the sixth filming's lake still 25 to 40% dark (shaded shallows about 77
+    /// against the reference's 130 to 139): the milk takes up to 0.5 of the bed's light (was
+    /// 0.38), in a paler green (was 0.4, 0.59, 0.5); lit by the hour's ambient and
+    /// [`MILK_KEEP`] of its sun even in shade (light scattered in from the lit water round it:
+    /// the sixth round lit it by the shaded light alone, so in shade the veil took the bed's light
+    /// and gave back less); the deep colour goes [`DEEP_MILK`] of the way to the milk; the water
+    /// takes [`CLEAR`] of the hour's absorption over its path, so the bed shows through more; the
+    /// mirror looking down is 0.1 (was 0.07), and [`SKY_KEEP`] of it holds in shade (the sky is
+    /// still mirrored there).
+    ///
+    /// Eighth round, the seventh filming's shallows 155 against the reference's ~130, pale and
+    /// sandy, the bed hidden: knobs ([`super::KNOBS`]). Its `lookwater show` stills told why: the
+    /// pale band lay 1.2 to 3.6 yd deep (153 to 158), over a dark bed (about 60) the water let
+    /// through at 0.5 to 0.65, so its light was the shallow milk colour's, brighter than the deep
+    /// water's (114); the bed was all but hidden only under the first half yard. So the veil takes
+    /// at most 0.35 of the bed's light (`milk`, was 0.5) and comes in fully only past
+    /// [`MILK_DEPTH`] of depth (`milk_depth`; none at the waterline, 0.74 of it at 0.8 yd), and the
+    /// shallows' milk colour is [`MILK_TONE`] as bright (`milk_tone`); `milk_deep` and `absorb`
+    /// hold [`DEEP_MILK`] and [`CLEAR`].
+    ///
+    /// Ninth round, the eighth filming's sweep of `milk` 0 to 0.7 at noon: the middle depths went
+    /// 104 to 149, the deep 105 to 124 and the shallows 96 to 129 (the middle depths' body is the
+    /// pale shallow colour, which the veil lays over a dark bed; the deep's is darker), so one
+    /// strength could not bring all three to the reference's ~120 to 130. The veil's strength is
+    /// now [`MILK_SHALLOW`] (`milk_shallow`) to [`MILK_AT_YD`]'s first depth, [`MILK_MID`]
+    /// (`milk_mid`) at its second and [`MILK_DEEP`] (`milk_deep`) from its third, turning
+    /// smoothly between them (by the colour's smoothed depth); `milk_depth` still holds it off
+    /// the first yard so the bed shows there. From the sweep's slopes (about 47, 64 and 27 of
+    /// luminance for the whole veil in the shallows, the middle and the deep): 0.65, 0.3 and 0.65
+    /// for shallows ~127, middle ~130 and deep ~122. `lookwater tune milk <v>` sets all three.
+    /// The eighth round's `milk_deep` (the deep colour's share of the milk's) is `deep_tint`.
+    pub const MILK_SHALLOW: f32 = 0.65;
+    pub const MILK_MID: f32 = 0.3;
+    pub const MILK_DEEP: f32 = 0.65;
+    pub const MILK_AT_YD: [f32; 3] = [1.0, 2.5, 4.5];
+    pub const MILK_YD: f32 = 0.9;
+    pub const MILK_RGB: [f32; 3] = [0.6, 0.76, 0.68];
+    pub const MILK_MIX: f32 = 0.85;
+    pub const MILK_KEEP: f32 = 0.85;
+    pub const MILK_DEPTH: f32 = 1.2;
+    pub const MILK_TONE: f32 = 0.85;
+    pub const DEEP_MILK: f32 = 0.3;
+    pub const CLEAR: f32 = 0.6;
+    pub const DEEP_FROM: f32 = 1.0;
+    pub const DEEP_AT: f32 = 5.0;
+    pub const F0: f32 = 0.1;
+    pub const SKY_KEEP: f32 = 0.8;
+    /// The clouds in the water's mirror: their edge this many times softer and blurred as if a
+    /// pixel spanned [`SKY_BLUR`] of a radian (only the broadest of their octaves left), so a
+    /// mirrored cloud's edge is a soft drift of light rather than the hard edge between pale and
+    /// dark patches the sixth filming showed (it stayed put on the screen as the camera moved: a
+    /// mirror of the sky, not of the bed).
+    pub const SKY_SOFT: f32 = 4.0;
+    pub const SKY_BLUR: f32 = 0.2;
+    /// The sun shadow on the water: it takes [`SHADOW`] of the hour's shade (the in-scatter and the
+    /// sky still light it), feathered over a disc of [`SHADOW_YD`] ([`SHADOW_TAPS`] taps), a point
+    /// off a slice falling through to the next and a slice's last share blending into it, as the
+    /// ground's do. The fifth filming's straight diagonal seam was the shade's hand-over between
+    /// two slices (`lookwater show shadow` drew it, `lookshadow 0` took it away).
+    pub const SHADOW: f32 = 0.5;
+    pub const SHADOW_YD: f32 = 3.5;
+    pub const SHADOW_TAPS: u32 = 12;
+    /// The moving light on the shallow bed: this multiple of `lookwater caustics`' strength, at
+    /// this multiple of its pace.
+    pub const CAUSTICS: f32 = 1.6;
+    pub const CAUSTICS_PACE: f32 = 3.0;
+    /// The shore's pale haze: this share of it (the milk veils the shallows already), spread over
+    /// this multiple of its depth, so no pale band runs along the shore.
+    pub const HAZE: f32 = 0.4;
+    pub const HAZE_SPREAD: f32 = 2.0;
+    /// The rain's drop rings on this surface (the sixth filming's rain showed streaks and no
+    /// rings: 0081's rings are 3 hundredths of a yard wide and fade out where a pixel spans 0.035
+    /// yd, about the films' camera): cells of [`RAIN_CELL_YD`], a ring growing to [`RAIN_REACH`]
+    /// of a cell, [`RAIN_WIDTH`] of a cell across, kept until a pixel spans [`RAIN_FADE_YD`]; it
+    /// tilts the surface and lays a pale line, at most [`RAIN_SHEEN`] of the water's light.
+    ///
+    /// Eighth round, no rings to see on the seventh filming (a pale line at 0.35 times the rain,
+    /// twice over, on pale water): knobs ([`super::KNOBS`]): the line is [`RAIN_SHEEN`] at a
+    /// ring's birth (`rain`), its crest's sun side brighter and its far side darker by
+    /// [`RAIN_RELIEF`] of that; cells of [`RAIN_CELL_YD`] (`rain_size`, rings to about 0.38 yd
+    /// across, 28 px on the films), [`RAIN_RATE`] rings a second a cell (`rain_rate`, was 1.5, so
+    /// each lasts long enough to see).
+    ///
+    /// Ninth round, the eighth filming's rings a little dense: a cell has drops at
+    /// [`RAIN_DENSITY`] of the eighth round's share (`rain_density`; 0.85 of the cells in full
+    /// rain, so 0.68 now).
+    pub const RAIN_CELL_YD: f32 = 0.9;
+    pub const RAIN_REACH: f32 = 0.42;
+    pub const RAIN_WIDTH: f32 = 0.1;
+    pub const RAIN_FADE_YD: [f32; 2] = [0.04, 0.1];
+    pub const RAIN_SHEEN: f32 = 0.8;
+    pub const RAIN_RELIEF: f32 = 0.3;
+    pub const RAIN_RATE: f32 = 1.0;
+    pub const RAIN_DENSITY: f32 = 0.8;
+    /// The fine grain (eighth round), per pixel in `liquid.wgsl` rather than in the map (whose
+    /// 0.05 yd texels cannot hold it): two octaves of the bake's gradient noise, [`GRAIN_YD`]
+    /// (`grain_yd`) and [`GRAIN_SECOND`] of it, weighted [`GRAIN_WEIGHT`], each a whole number of
+    /// cells over the tile, drifting along [`GRAIN_DIR`], each faded out as a pixel grows from
+    /// [`GRAIN_FADE`] of its wavelength to the second; it shades the water by [`GRAIN`] (`grain`)
+    /// at its mean tilt toward the sun, tilts the surface by that share of the hour's slope, and
+    /// bends the bed.
+    ///
+    /// Ninth round: the eighth round's drift (0.3 and 0.25 yd/s, a capillary ripple's own pace)
+    /// moved the octaves a tenth and a seventh of their wavelength every 30th of a second, along
+    /// crossing ways, so the grain boiled: its frame-to-frame change on film was 1.6 times its
+    /// texture (the reference's, a quarter). It drifts at [`GRAIN_SPEED`] yd/s now
+    /// (`grain_speed`), the second octave at [`GRAIN_SPEED_SHARE`] of it, near still, so its
+    /// texture can rise to the reference's: [`GRAIN`] 0.45 (was 0.25; the sweep's 0.35 gave a
+    /// fine texture of 3.4 and 0.5 gave 5.3, against the reference's 6.4 to 9).
+    pub const GRAIN: f32 = 0.45;
+    pub const GRAIN_YD: f32 = 0.1;
+    pub const GRAIN_SECOND: f32 = 0.6;
+    pub const GRAIN_WEIGHT: [f32; 2] = [1.0, 0.7];
+    pub const GRAIN_SPEED: f32 = 0.04;
+    pub const GRAIN_SPEED_SHARE: [f32; 2] = [1.0, 0.85];
+    pub const GRAIN_DIR: [[f32; 2]; 2] = [[0.8, 0.6], [-0.6, 0.8]];
+    pub const GRAIN_FADE: [f32; 2] = [0.25, 0.6];
+    /// The 0.36 yd layer's share of its weight (`mid`): it read as the embossed relief.
+    pub const MID: f32 = 0.5;
+    /// The mirror and the sun's highlight off this surface, a multiple of the tuned (`sky`,
+    /// `sheen`): the reference's water is flat and clear, a little less of the sky's sheen.
+    pub const SKY: f32 = 0.85;
+    pub const SHEEN: f32 = 1.0;
+}
